		</control>

		<!-- the row: PS5 rhythm, 110px tiles with 10px gaps, focused 172px.
		     FIXEDLIST, not list, and that is the whole character of the row:
		     the selector does not travel, the tiles slide into it. A plain
		     list walks the highlight along stationary tiles until it reaches
		     the edge and only then scrolls, which reads as a cursor on a
		     grid. A console reads as a reel: one lit position, and the
		     content moving through it.
		     focusposition 0 puts that lit position at the head of the row, so
		     games arrive from the right and none is ever half off the left
		     edge.
		     MEASURED, not assumed: Container(9000).Position stays 0 while
		     CurrentItem climbs, which is the whole property this control
		     exists to have.
		     It also WRAPS - right from the last item lands back on Library.
		     That is not something this change introduced a decision about,
		     and consoles do the opposite: they stop at the end and make the
		     dead end audible. Open question for Donnie, not a silent choice. -->
		<control type="fixedlist" id="9000">
			<!-- left 65, not 96, and every x inside the layouts below is +31 to
			     compensate - so no tile moves on screen. The 31px is HEADROOM: a
			     list clips to its own bounds, and the focused item's halo is drawn
			     31px left of its tile. At left 96 the first item's halo was sliced
			     flat against the edge and its caption cut with it (Donnie, 8 Aug:
			     "it's clipping on the left side"). Width grows by the same 31 so
			     the right edge is where it always was. -->
			<!-- left 80 = the FOCUSED TILE's own left edge, on purpose: the
			     container clips its items at this bound, so the outgoing tile
			     slides UNDER the selector ring and is guillotined exactly at the
			     ring's stroke - a clip aligned with a drawn border reads as
			     intentional, the same way a screen-edge clip does. This replaced
			     the scrim-over-artwork fade, which Donnie rightly called a smudge:
			     a dark overlay on live art is not an opacity fade, and Kodi has no
			     per-item opacity to do a real one. The halo rings are STATIC
			     overlays drawn after this list (the selector never moves, so they
			     never need to) - which is also what frees the row from needing
			     left headroom inside the container at all. -->
			<!-- left 116, not 80. THE POINT OF THE WHOLE RESTRUCTURE: a list
			     clips to its own bounds, and with the bound at the focused
			     tile's left edge the outgoing item's resting tile still had
			     36px inside it (local -74..36 after the scroll) - the second
			     copy that crawled in the ring while the ghost flew away.
			     Move the bound to 116 and drop every layout x by 36 and that
			     remnant lands entirely LEFT of the bound, so it is clipped to
			     nothing. Nothing on screen moves: the tiles are where they
			     were, the container just stops drawing 36px sooner.
			     This is only possible because the focus frame is no longer
			     INSIDE the list - see the hoisted frame below. -->
			<left>116</left>
			<top>76</top>
			<width>1774</width>
			<height>290</height>
			<orientation>horizontal</orientation>
			<focusposition>0</focusposition>
			<scrolltime tween="sine" easing="out">210</scrolltime>
			<onup>9101</onup>
			<ondown>9500</ondown>
			<itemlayout width="120" height="290">
				<control type="image">
					<!-- 10, not 46: the container bound moved +36, so every x in
					     here drops by 36 and each tile stays at the screen x it
					     has always had. 10 is also exactly what makes the
					     outgoing slot (local -120) put its tile at -110..0 -
					     flush with the bound, so none of it survives the clip. -->
					<left>10</left>
					<top>48</top>
					<width>110</width>
					<height>110</height>
					<texture background="true" diffuse="couch/mask-round.png">$INFO[ListItem.Art(thumb)]</texture>
					<aspectratio>scale</aspectratio>
					<colordiffuse>c8ffffff</colordiffuse>
				</control>
			</itemlayout>
			<!-- SAME WIDTH as itemlayout, deliberately. Kodi sizes a slot from its
			     layout, and focusedlayout being 62px wider meant the row's current
			     item kept a fat slot even after the row lost focus - a visible hole
			     between two tiles (Donnie, 8 Aug: "a weird gap between bb and hk").
			     With both at 120 the rhythm never changes and the focused tile grows
			     OVER its neighbours instead, which is what the halo (232px wide in a
			     182px slot) has always done anyway. Everything below is re-centred on
			     itemlayout's tile centre (85,103) so it expands symmetrically about
			     where the resting tile already sits. -->
			<!-- 151, not 120. The unfocused tile sits at slot-local 61..171 in a
			     120 slot, which is what makes the 110px/10px rhythm. The FOCUSED
			     tile is 172 wide at slot-local 30..202, so in a 120 slot it
			     overflowed 82px and drew straight over its neighbour - visible as
			     the next game losing its left edge. 151 puts the following tile at
			     151+61=212, ten past the focused tile's 202, so the gap either
			     side of the selector matches every other gap in the row.
			     Equal widths were a deliberate earlier fix ("one slot width, so
			     the row has no hole where focus used to be") - that hole cannot
			     happen now the selector is pinned at the head and never moves. -->
			<!-- IDENTICAL to itemlayout, and that is the whole idea now. The
			     focus frame - big tile, lightbar, caption - is drawn OUTSIDE
			     this list (below), so the row itself only ever draws resting
			     tiles. That is what lets the container bound sit clear of the
			     exit region, and it is how a console row is actually built:
			     one lit position, content moving through it, rather than a
			     tile that swells in place. -->
			<focusedlayout width="120" height="290">
				<control type="image">
					<left>10</left>
					<top>48</top>
					<width>110</width>
					<height>110</height>
					<texture background="true" diffuse="couch/mask-round.png">$INFO[ListItem.Art(thumb)]</texture>
					<aspectratio>scale</aspectratio>
					<colordiffuse>c8ffffff</colordiffuse>
				</control>
			</focusedlayout>
			<!-- The trailing $INFO is a REVISION, not decoration. Kodi caches a
			     plugin listing for the life of the container, and Container.Refresh
			     is a no-op on Home (this window is KEEP_IN_MEMORY) - so a game that
			     was paused or quit kept its old row until the player left and came
			     back. Measured: after quitting, the tile still read "- paused" and
			     the card still drew a freeze-frame whose file had been deleted 164ms
			     earlier. A container DOES rebuild when its resolved content string
			     changes, so pause-snap stamps CouchGamesRev on every capture and
			     every clear and the row follows. Empty property = empty suffix, so
			     the URL stays valid before anything has ever been stamped. -->
			<content target="videos">plugin://script.copacetic.helper/?info=games&amp;home=1&amp;v=8$INFO[Window(Home).Property(CouchGamesRev),&amp;r=]</content>
		</control>

		<!-- THE EXIT GHOST - the tile that actually leaves. Donnie, 11 Aug:
		     "go back to the way it worked when it left the box".
		     THE LIST CANNOT DO THIS. A fixedlist gives an outgoing item exactly
		     one slot of travel (120px) and then culls it, and the container
		     clips it at its own left bound long before that. The tile needs
		     236px of travel just to clear the screen and it only ever gets 120.
		     So the thing you watch fly off the edge is NOT the list's tile: it
		     is this control, drawn OUTSIDE the container where nothing clips it
		     and no slot limit applies, textured with the item the row just left
		     ($INFO ListItem(-1) - verified live: with Bloodborne current it
		     resolves Hollow Knight's composed tile).
		     WHY <visible> IS OnNext AND THE ANIMATION IS *Hidden*, which is the
		     whole trick and cost an evening to find: Container(9000).OnNext is
		     a ONE-FRAME PULSE. Measured by recording a ghost gated on it with
		     no animation - visible for exactly 1 frame out of 420, and it does
		     NOT scale with scrolltime. So OnNext can TRIGGER an animation (a
		     Focus/Unfocus condition is read once, at trigger time, which is why
		     the old dissolve ever ran) but it can never GATE a 210ms flight:
		     the first attempt used it as <visible> with a Visible animation and
		     the ghost was simply never seen. Kodi keeps a control rendered
		     while its HIDDEN animation plays, so the control is visible for the
		     one pulse frame and then flies out over the full 210ms after the
		     condition has already gone false.
		     Drawn BEFORE the halo rings, so it passes behind the selector
		     stroke on its way out rather than over it. No fade: fading is what
		     made the old exit read as a smudge, and it does not need one -
		     it travels 250px and is gone off the left edge.
		     Measured at 10x: tracks steadily left at ~3.5 skin px/frame, fully
		     clear of the screen. Real-speed flight 183ms. -->
		<control type="image" id="9010">
			<left>80</left>
			<top>101</top>
			<width>156</width>
			<height>156</height>
			<texture background="true" diffuse="couch/mask-round.png">$INFO[Container(9000).ListItem(-1).Art(thumb)]</texture>
			<aspectratio>scale</aspectratio>
			<visible>Container(9000).OnNext</visible>
			<animation effect="slide" start="0,0" end="-250,0" time="210" tween="sine" easing="out">Hidden</animation>
		</control>

		<!-- THE FOCUS FRAME, hoisted out of the list (11 Aug, "can we do the
		     proper fix"). It used to live in focusedlayout, which meant the
		     container had to be wide enough to draw it - and a container that
		     is wide enough to draw the focused tile is also wide enough to
		     show 36px of the tile that just left. That was the double image.
		     Out here nothing clips it, so the list's bound can sit at 116 and
		     eat the remnant whole.
		     It does not grow any more, and that is the trade Donnie took: the
		     frame is fixed and the artwork flows THROUGH it (fadetime does the
		     crossfade), which is the reel the row comment has described since
		     day one - one lit position, content moving through it - rather
		     than a tile that swells in place.
		     Screen coords, straight from where these controls used to render:
		     tile 80,101 (container top 76 + layout top 25), lightbar 80,267,
		     caption 95,291. Drawn AFTER the exit ghost so the incoming art
		     covers it at rest and the outgoing tile emerges from behind the
		     frame, and BEFORE the halo rings so the rings still sit on top. -->
		<control type="image">
			<left>80</left>
			<top>101</top>
			<width>156</width>
			<height>156</height>
			<texture background="true" diffuse="couch/mask-round.png">$INFO[Container(9000).ListItem.Art(thumb)]</texture>
			<aspectratio>scale</aspectratio>
			<fadetime>0</fadetime>
			<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
			<!-- launch flourish, moved with the frame it belongs to -->
			<animation effect="zoom" start="100" end="150" center="236,179" time="480" tween="cubic" easing="out" condition="!String.IsEmpty(Window(home).Property(CouchLaunching))">Conditional</animation>
			<animation effect="fade" start="100" end="0" time="480" tween="sine" easing="in" condition="!String.IsEmpty(Window(home).Property(CouchLaunching))">Conditional</animation>
		</control>
		<control type="image">
			<left>80</left>
			<top>267</top>
			<width>156</width>
			<height>12</height>
			<texture colordiffuse="$VAR[Couch_Live_Color]">couch/lightbar.png</texture>
			<aspectratio>stretch</aspectratio>
			<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
		</control>
		<control type="label">
			<left>95</left>
			<top>291</top>
			<width>620</width>
			<height>38</height>
			<font>Couch_Label</font>
			<textcolor>ffeceef1</textcolor>
			<label>$INFO[Container(9000).ListItem.Label]</label>
			<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
		</control>

		<!-- The selector, STATIC. The focus box never moves (fixedlist,
		     focusposition 0), so the rings do not belong inside the per-item
		     layout - hoisting them out is what lets the container clip
		     outgoing tiles at the ring's own stroke. Screen coords: the ring
		     sits 30px outside the 156px tile at 80..236, so 50..266; zoom
		     centre (236,179) is the tile's shared right edge in screen space,
		     the same continuity sum as the tile's own grow. -->
		<control type="image">
			<left>50</left>
			<top>71</top>
			<width>216</width>
			<height>216</height>
			<texture border="48" infill="false" colordiffuse="$VAR[Couch_Live_Color]">couch/halo-glow.png</texture>
			<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
			<animation effect="zoom" start="70.5" end="100" center="236,179" time="170" tween="sine" easing="out">Focus</animation>
			<animation effect="zoom" start="100" end="70.5" center="236,179" time="120" tween="sine" easing="out">Unfocus</animation>
		</control>
		<control type="image">
			<left>50</left>
			<top>71</top>
			<width>216</width>
			<height>216</height>
			<texture border="48" infill="false" colordiffuse="dcffffff">couch/halo-core.png</texture>
			<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
			<animation effect="zoom" start="70.5" end="100" center="236,179" time="170" tween="sine" easing="out">Focus</animation>
			<animation effect="zoom" start="100" end="70.5" center="236,179" time="120" tween="sine" easing="out">Unfocus</animation>
		</control>

		<!-- lower-left: the focused game's own logo, PS5 style; text fallback
		     when Steam has no separate logo for it -->
		<control type="image">
			<left>96</left>
			<top>520</top>
			<width>560</width>
			<height>240</height>
			<texture background="true">$INFO[Container(9000).ListItem.Art(clearlogo)]</texture>
			<aspectratio align="left" aligny="bottom">keep</aspectratio>
			<fadetime>300</fadetime>
			<visible>!$EXP[Home_Library_Focused] + !String.IsEmpty(Container(9000).ListItem.Art(clearlogo))</visible>
